SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING - THE WEEK AHEAD
Sunday, March 1, 2026

What we are watching this week.

Monday: The Port of Los Angeles and Long Beach release February volumes. Expect a soft month after a front-loaded January; the interesting number is empties, which tell you whether carriers are repositioning for a spring bump.

Tuesday: The STB holds its second public hearing on the rail merger application. Shipper groups have filed 400 pages of comments; the railroads have filed 900. Watch the questions on reciprocal switching, not the opening statements.

Wednesday: Comment deadline for the proposed changes to the hours-of-service split-sleeper provision. Trucking groups support it; safety groups do not; the agency has signaled it wants a pilot rather than a rule.

Thursday: TPM, the big transpacific conference, wraps in Long Beach. Contract rate gossip will leak all week. Our read from the hallways so far: shippers are asking for flat, carriers are asking for up 10%, and both sides know the answer is down.

Friday: The February jobs report. Transportation and warehousing employment has been flat for fourteen months; the wage line is what to read.

Also this week: a big-box retailer's investor day, a Panama Canal draft announcement for the dry season, and the annual pallet association meeting, which we are attending so you do not have to.

See you tomorrow at 6.

